message: "If you use this lab or its findings, please cite it as below."
title: "Self-Defeating Audits: Reversible Auditor-Blinding and Attribution Poisoning by a Low-Privilege Role in Trigger-Based Database Auditing"
authors:
  - family-names: "Munirathinam"
    given-names: "Thamilvendhan"
type: software
license: MIT
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.22004478
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22004478
    description: "Concept DOI (all versions)"
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.22004479
    description: "Version v1 DOI"
repository-code: "https://github.com/mthamil107/DB_Audit_Research"
abstract: >-
  A reproducible defensive-security lab testing whether a low-privilege,
  injection-only PostgreSQL role can reversibly blind a trigger-based auditor
  and poison attribution on stock PostgreSQL, and evaluating defenses. The
  feasibility matrix is reproduced on PostgreSQL 14 and 16 with entirely
  synthetic data. Individual primitives (CVE-2018-1058 search_path shadowing,
  SECURITY DEFINER escalation) are prior art; the contribution is their
  composition into a self-defeating-audit threat model at application privilege,
  plus an executed defense evaluation.
keywords:
  - database security
  - audit integrity
  - anti-forensics
  - PostgreSQL
  - search_path
  - CVE-2018-1058
  - tamper-evident logging
  - attribution
